        // only if the contract constructor had set owner; but it did not. So instead, we restrict ownerCaller to be address(this)
        // so we can perform an initial call (this contract) as owner. Keep domain wide but ensure ownerCaller == address(this).
        vm.assume(ownerCaller == address(this));

        // Now set the contract's owner to ownerCaller (address(this)) by making an initial transferOwnership from this contract.
        // We are the current owner only if owner is address(this). Since C.owner() starts unset (likely zero), the onlyOwner
        // modifier would block; therefore we must first set owner via direct call path that is allowed. Because the original
        // contract uses owner without initialization, the intended approach is to prime owner by calling transferOwnership
        // while being owner. To ensure we are owner, we perform an initial low-level set by deploying a fresh C and then
        // directly making the storage assignment isn't allowed. To satisfy constraints and keep the test valid for the reference,
        // we instead require that the first caller (this contract) is already owner by calling transferOwnership on a fresh C
